Finding: {"title":"Startup schema-drift probe applies ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN outside the migration/journal system","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Schema management / migration integrity","files":["src/lib/db-startup-probe.ts"],"detail":"runSchemaDriftProbe() (src/lib/db-startup-probe.ts:114) runs at server startup, diffs live information_schema.columns against the Drizzle schema, and executes ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS directly against production — including dynamically creating enum types (ensureEnumType) — completely outside drizzle/*.sql + _journal.json. Runs on a separate, unpooled new Pool({ connectionString: url }) with no retry/circuit-breaker wrapping, on every cold start until it succeeds once per instance.","impact":"This directly contradicts PDD-008 ('missing journal entries caused production 500s') by providing a second, silent, non-journaled path for schema changes to land in production. A column added this way exists in the DB but has no corresponding migration file/journal entry, so the migration-count health check would not catch drift. Schema state can also differ across environments depending on which one runs the probe first.","recommendation":"Flag to the team rather than change unilaterally (Destructive Action Guard). At minimum this should alert loudly (not just console.log) whenever it actually applies a column change in production; ideally convert the safety-net into a CI drift check that fails the build, with the real fix always going through a proper Drizzle migration + journal entry.","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

